If you have more context or information on how to evaluate general relativity, I may be able to help.', 'status': 'success', 'error': None, 'token_usage': 3517, 'chunks': [{'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 85, 'text': 'evaluating the background-independence of their construction, it may be useful to first note a few more general points regarding semiclassical approaches to quantum gravity. Semiclassical gravity means that we treat the gravitational field classically while we take the matter fields to be quantum. This is a good approximation in the limit G →0, where the metric and the matter fields are decoupled, which allows us to define the quan- tum matter fields on some fixed solution to the Einstein field equations. Additionally, we can consider small linearized perturbations of the metric and quantize these (see e.g. Kiefer, 2007). Then the quantum fields also include free gravitons. Formally, the semiclassical approximation to quantum gravity may be characterized as a perturbative expansion of the Wheeler-DeWitt equation in powers of Newton’s constant G (see e.g. Singh and Padmanabhan, 1989; Kiefer, 1994).33 The leading con- tribution is of order 1/G: at this order, one recovers the Hamilton-Jacobi equation for a free gravitational field, which can be used to derive the Einstein equations (Gerlach, 1969). The next order, O(G0), gives the functional Schr¨odinger equation for quantum fields in curved spacetime. Here the metric obtained at O(1/G) can be used as an input to describe the'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 122, 'text': 'supported by the John Templeton Foundation grant 63670 on ‘Emergence of time from chaos in operator algebras’. SDH also thanks Jesus College, Oxford for their hospitality and support during the completion of this paper. Bibliography Anderson, J. L. (1967). Principles of Relativity Physics. Academic Press, New York/London. Bak, D., Kim, C., and Yi, S.-H. (2024). Janus deformation of de Sitter space and transitions in gravitational algebras. Journal of High Energy Physics, 2024(11):94. Belot, G. (2011). Background-independence. General Relativity and Gravitation, 43(10):2865–2884. Bergmann, P. G. (1949). Non-Linear Field Theories. Physical Review, 75(4):680–685. Bisognano, J. J. and Wichmann, E. H. (1975). On the duality condition for a Hermitian scalar field. Journal of Mathematical Physics, 16(4):985–1007. Bisognano, J. J. and Wichmann, E. H. (1976). On the duality condition for quantum fields. Journal of Mathematical Physics, 17(3):303–321. Blommaert, A., Kudler-Flam, J., and Urbach, E. Y. (2025). Absolute entropy and the observer’s no-boundary state. arXiv:2505.14771 [hep-th]. Borchers, H. J. (1964). Field operators as Cınfty functions in spacelike directions. Il Nuovo Cimento (1955-1965), 33(6):1600–1613. Bousso, R. (2000). Positive vacuum energy and the N-bound. Journal of High Energy Physics, 2000(11):038. 38 Butterfield, J. and Isham, C. J. (1999). Spacetime and the Philosophical Challenge of Quantum Gravity. In'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22097', 'title': 'Operational Limits of Gravity-Mediated Entanglement', 'chunk_index': 4, 'text': 'a strictly local classical mediator in the usual BMV setting, but it would not automatically settle every possible question about semiclassical, hybrid, or postquantum descriptions of gravity. For this reason, several works have examined ∗haddadi@ipm.ir what such experiments can and cannot certify. Classical- channel and hybrid-dynamics models have clarified the assumptions under which entanglement witnesses become meaningful [7, 8]. Relativistic and field-theoretic analyses have also shown that the claim must be phrased carefully, especially when one asks what is meant by the gravita- tional mediator and which degrees of freedom are being tested [9, 10]. More recent studies have further discussed measurement choices, hidden-variable descriptions, and the distinction between observing entanglement and ex- cluding broader classes of classical explanations [11–13]. The present work follows this cautious viewpoint. We do not claim that one witness proves a complete the- ory of quantum gravity. Instead, we ask which opera- tional signatures remain available once finite wave-packet width, dephasing, and residual force systematics are in- cluded [14, 15]. The experimental motivation for this question has be- come stronger in recent years. Levitated optomechanics has made the preparation and control of massive quan- tum motion a realistic research direction [16, 17]. Feed- back cooling,'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 86, 'text': 'can be used to derive the Einstein equations (Gerlach, 1969). The next order, O(G0), gives the functional Schr¨odinger equation for quantum fields in curved spacetime. Here the metric obtained at O(1/G) can be used as an input to describe the dynamics of quantum fields on a fixed background. Higher-order cor- rections include backreaction effects of the quantum fields on the metric, such as those described by the semiclassical Einstein field equations: Gµν = 8πG ⟨Tµν⟩, (24) which can be derived from the perturbative expansion of the Wheeler-DeWitt equation at O(G). So at this point, the metric becomes dynamical and the DPMs of the theory should be consistent with Eq. (24). If the theory includes free gravitons, these should contribute to the stress-energy tensor, since they are quantum fields.34 How does this relate to CLPW’s algebraic framework? Recall that, as we noted towards the end of Section 2.4, in their construction, perturbations of order O(1/G) affect the size of the Hilbert space. This reflects the fact that every Hilbert space representation of the algebra is associated to a particular fixed solution of the Einstein field equations. Within every such Hilbert space, states describe O(1) perturbations around the input state. So the'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 87, 'text': 'space. This reflects the fact that every Hilbert space representation of the algebra is associated to a particular fixed solution of the Einstein field equations. Within every such Hilbert space, states describe O(1) perturbations around the input state. So the Hilbert space describes the dynamics of quantum fields on a fixed spacetime background. Backreaction effects are not taken into account in CLPW’s construction. 33This is analogous to a Born-Oppenheimer approximation, where M ∼1/G takes the role of the large nucleus mass and the mass of the matter fields m the role of the electron mass. 34To include gravitons, we write the metric that is used in the Wheeler-DeWitt equations as gµν = ˆgµν + √ 32πG hµν, where ˆgµν is fixed and hµν satisfies the linearized Einstein equations. hµν will then appear at the same footing as the matter fields, see Kiefer (1994). 27 Before we continue to evaluate the algebraic framework with respect to current no- tions of background-independence in the philosophical literature, we should emphasize that we should not expect the theory, like any semiclassical theory of gravity, to fully satisfy the conditions for background-independence. These definitions all formulated some way in which the background has to be'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 108, 'text': 'emerges from the perspective of the observer is captured by the same equation. Also, as we discussed in Section 2.5, the hypothesis is that for any background the algebra is of Type II and includes a notion of entropy and density matrices. So far we have left backreaction effects completely out of the picture and worked in the strict G →0 limit. However, we may now wonder how robust the background- independent algebra and the general statements that we derived from it are against perturbative corrections in G. When G is non-zero, both the observer and the quantum fields backreact on the metric, and are thus coupled to each other. This means that the Hamiltonian constraint in Eq. (11) that we used to gravitationally dress the algebra to the worldline of the observer is no longer a simple sum. So the algebra is potentially deformed by O(G) corrections. However, as Witten (2024) argues, the important point here is that the algebra that we constructed in the G →0 limit is a factor, meaning that it has a trivial centre—which has a crucial consequence: factors are rigid, i.e. they have no infinitesimal deformations (in this sense, a factor can be seen'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 40, 'text': 'is weakly coupled to matter and can be treated perturbatively. Following the arguments made in the introduction, we will explicitly include the observer as a quantum degree of freedom in the construc- tion, and require that the algebra is independent of the state of both the spacetime and matter fields and the state of the observer itself. The setup follows Witten (2025), and consists of a worldline γ : R →M, describing the trajectory of an observer travelling along a geodesic on a manifold M. We focus on the algebra of matter fields that the observer can measure along γ and we make no assumptions about the background manifold except that it is globally hyperbolic. For simplicity, we restrict our attention to a single scalar field ϕ(x) that the observer can measure along its worldline γ. Let τ ∈R be the observer’s proper time that parametrises γ, then ϕ(τ) ≡ϕ(γ(τ)) denotes the field at that point along γ. These will be the building blocks used to construct our algebra including the observer. However, since only differences in proper time between two spacetime points have physical significance, the fields should be invariant under reparametrisations of the form: τ →τ + c'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 133, 'text': 'Trieste, Italy. World Scientific. Witten, E. (1996). Reflections on the Fate of Spacetime. Physics Today, 49(4):24–30. Witten, E. (2022a). Gravity and the crossed product. Journal of High Energy Physics, 2022(10):8. 43 Witten, E. (2022b). Why Does Quantum Field Theory In Curved Spacetime Make Sense? And What Happens To The Algebra of Observables In The Thermodynamic Limit? In Ge, M.-L. and He, Y.-H., editors, Dialogues Between Physics and Mathematics: C. N. Yang at 100. Springer International Publishing, Cham. arXiv:2112.11614. Witten, E. (2024). Algebras, regions, and observers. In Donagi, R., Langer, A., Su lkowski, P., and Wendland, K., editors, Proceedings of Symposia in Pure Mathemat- ics, volume 107, pages 247–276, Warsaw, Poland. American Mathematical Society. Witten, E. (2025). A Background Independent Algebra in Quantum Gravity. General Relativity and Gravitation, 57(1):17. 44'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 124, 'text': 'Davies, P. C. W. (1975). Scalar production in Schwarzschild and Rindler metrics. Journal of Physics A: Mathematical and General, 8(4):609. De Haro, S. (2017). Dualities and emergent gravity: Gauge/gravity duality. Studies in History and Philosophy of Science Part B: Studies in History and Philosophy of Modern Physics, 59:109–125. De Haro, S., Mayerson, D. R., and Butterfield, J. N. (2016). Conceptual Aspects of Gauge/Gravity Duality. Foundations of Physics, 46(11):1381–1425. De Vuyst, J., Eccles, S., H¨ohn, P. A., and Kirklin, J. (2025). Gravitational entropy is observer-dependent. Journal of High Energy Physics, 7(146):1–31. Doplicher, S., Haag, R., and Roberts, J. E. (1969). Fields, observables and gauge trans- formations II. Communications in Mathematical Physics, 15(3):173–200. Earman, J. (2011). The Unruh effect for philosophers. Studies in History and Philosophy of Science Part B: Studies in History and Philosophy of Modern Physics, 42(2):81–97. Fulling, S. A. (1973). Nonuniqueness of Canonical Field Quantization in Riemannian Space-Time. Physical Review D, 7(10):2850–2862. Gelfand, I. and Neumark, M. (1943). On the imbedding of normed rings into the ring of operators in Hilbert space. Rec. Math. [Mat. Sbornik] N.S., 54(2):197–217. Gerlach, U. H. (1969). Derivation of the Ten Einstein Field Equations from the Semiclas- sical Approximation to Quantum Geometrodynamics. Physical Review,'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22042', 'title': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence', 'chunk_index': 1, 'text': 'On Algebraic Background-Independence Luka Vinck1,2 and Sebastian De Haro1,3 1Institute of Physics, University of Amsterdam, The Netherlands 2Dutch Institute for Emergent Phenomena, The Netherlands 3Institute for Logic, Language and Computation, University of Amsterdam, The Netherlands October, 2026 Abstract It has recently been claimed that algebraic methods can be used to give a background-independent description of semiclassical gravity. This paper aims to provide an entry point into recent algebraic approaches to quantum gravity and to analyse and critically assess the conception of background-independence here involved. We discuss an algebraic framework for formulating an algebra of observ- ables along the worldline of an observer in a cosmological setting. By ‘gravitationally dressing’ the observables to the observer’s worldline, the reference to the spacetime background can be set aside at the level of the abstract *-algebra. Thus this *- algebra is taken to be independent of any particular spacetime realisation, with different states on the algebra corresponding to different backgrounds. We compare this algebraic framework to three influential notions of background-independence in the literature and argue that none of these fully captures the sense in which the *-algebra is taken to be background-independent. Building on this, we extract a novel notion of algebraic background-independence'}]}

{'answer': 'The difference between AC and DC current is that AC (alternating current) changes direction and magnitude periodically, whereas DC (direct current) flows in one direction only. In an AC circuit, the current changes direction at the same frequency as the alternating voltage, whereas in a DC circuit, the current flows in one direction only.

In the context of the provided text, the experiment demonstrates the skin effect, where the AC resistance is higher than the DC resistance in a conductor. This is because the AC current redistributes itself unevenly within the conductor, resulting in a higher current density near the surface that decays exponentially towards the core. This spatial redistribution effectively reduces the usable cross-sectional area, leading to an increase in AC resistance.

The experiment shows that the non-magnetic stainless steel exhibits a similar AC and DC resistance, whereas the ferromagnetic structural steel exhibits a pronounced AC impedance surge at line frequency, indicating the role of permeability in driving the skin effect.', 'status': 'success', 'error': None, 'token_usage': 3456, 'chunks': [{'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 36, 'text': 'mean free path exceeds the skin depth (anomalous skin effect) – none of which apply to the present experiment. Equation (S1) holds locally for both DC and the quasistationary AC regime, as electron relaxation times in metals are orders of magnitude faster than the field period. Technical metals typically feature exceptionally high electrical conductivity. Even at megahertz frequencies, the displacement current density⃗jd = ε0εr∂⃗E/∂t (with ε0 being the vacuum permittivity and εr the relative permittivity) is negligible compared to the conduction current density (Equation (S1)): ⃗ jd⃗ j = ωε0εr σ ≪1 . (S2) Under this quasistationary approximation, Maxwell’s equations for the electric field⃗E and the magnetic flux density⃗B in a linear, homogeneous, charge-free conductor reduce to: ∇×⃗E = −∂⃗B ∂t , ∇×⃗B = µ0µrσ⃗E , ∇·⃗E = 0 , ∇·⃗B = 0 . (S3) Taking the curl of Faraday’s law and applying ∇× (∇×⃗E) = ∇(∇·⃗E) −∇2⃗E yields the governing field equation inside the conductor: ∇2⃗E = µ0µrσ ∂⃗E ∂t . (S4) Equation (S4) is a parabolic diffusion equation rather than a hyperbolic wave equation (which contains a second-order time derivative, ∂2⃗E/∂t2). This mathematical transition directly reflects the difference in how electromagnetic energy interacts with matter. In non-conducting dielectrics,'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 8, 'text': 'macroscopic voltage U and current I. In the DC case, this yields the familiar macroscopic Ohm’s law, U = RDCI , with RDC = ρ l A (3) as the static resistance of a homogeneous conductor of length l and uniform cross-sectional area A. For a solid cylindrical conductor of outer radius r0, the conductive area is given by A = πr2 0. In introductory physics courses, Equation (3) is often introduced empirically. Commercial setups similar to ours are widely used to measure small DC resistances and demonstrate how RDC varies 2 with geometry and material [16]. However, because they are traditionally equipped with non-magnetic specimens (e.g., copper or brass), they are generally not applied to investigate AC phenomena such as the skin effect. Under AC excitation at frequency f (ω = 2πf), time-varying magnetic fields induce internal eddy currents that oppose current flow in the conductor core and reinforce it near the boundary. Consequently,⃗j(⃗r, t) becomes spatially non-uniform, restricting current transport to a thin surface layer. Electrodynamically, this current redistribution and the resulting internal inductance transform the static resistance into a frequency-dependent complex internal impedance: ZAC(ω) = RAC(ω) + i ωLint(ω) . (4) Here, RAC ≥RDC accounts for enhanced'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 22, 'text': 'impact of magnetic permeability on the skin effect The AC experiments (diamond symbols in Figure 2) compare the electrical response of the solid rods under 50 Hz AC excitation. While the AC characteristics remain linear through the origin, their slope reflects the magnitude of the complex AC impedance |ZAC| rather than pure static resistance (Equation (5)), with results listed in Table 3. For non-magnetic stainless steel (µr ≈1), the AC and DC characteristics collapse onto a single line within experimental uncertainty (|ZAC|/RDC = 0.998(5)). At 50 Hz, the calculated skin depth (δ ≈64 mm) vastly exceeds the rod radius (r0 = 6.00 mm). Consequently, current density remains uniform throughout the cross-section. In this quasistatic regime (x ≪1), the inductive reactance is 6 negligible compared to the ohmic resistance, yielding |ZAC| ≈RAC ≈RDC, as expected based on Equation (8). In terms of electrical transport, the conductor thus behaves identically under AC and DC conditions. In sharp contrast, ferromagnetic structural steel (µr ≫1) shows a clear increase in slope under AC conditions. Although structural steel is the superior DC conductor (with a static resistivity lower than that of stainless steel by about a factor of 4, see Table 2), its AC impedance'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 25, 'text': '(thus confirming the asymptotic trend estimated above), along with ωLint/RDC ≈1.56, δ ≈1.5 mm and µr ≈450. Note that for ferromagnetics, µr is not a static material constant, but an effective, operating-point value dependent on the applied current I and the resulting internal magnetic field strength. The experimentally derived effective permeability is significantly lower than typical DC literature nominals (µr ∼103). This discrepancy can be primarily attributed to nonlinear hysteresis and skin-effect-induced eddy currents under AC conditions. A closer inspection of the linear fit to the AC U(I) characteristics of structural steel reveals small, systematic deviations: data points at lower currents (I ≤1.4 A) lie slightly below the fit, whereas those at higher currents (I ≥2.4 A) trend slightly above it. This behaviour reflects the current-dependent nature of the effective permeability in the un-saturated regime. As the current amplitude increases, the progressive alignment of magnetic domains enhances the magnetic response, causing the local slope (and thus the AC impedance) to increase. While core losses and hysteresis effects also play a role at elevated field strengths, this current-dependent stiffening of the effective permeability accounts for the observed slight upward curvature. 5 Pedagogical design and implementation To maximize conceptual learning and engagement,'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.10631', 'title': 'From Cycle Space to Cycle Manifold: Limits and Achievability of Blind False Data Injection Attacks', 'chunk_index': 6, 'text': 'physical constraints for both DC and AC power models. We reveal that the cycle structure is the fundamental component for both AC and AC models. In the DC model, the FDIA is constrained by the weighted cycle space. In the AC model, the FDIA is constrained by the cycle manifold. We prove that the weighted cycle space and cycle manifold are sufficient and necessary conditions to perform a complete FDIA for DC and AC models, respectively. We therefore study the limits and achievability of blind FDIA using these physical constraints. With these theories and studies, we not only provide a new tool for FDIA research but also offer new insights into power grid planning and protection. The main contributions are as follows: • Physical origin of FDIA stealth. We prove that the weighted cycle space is the exact 1 orthogonal comple- ment of the complete attack space in the DC model. For the AC model, we propose the theory of cycle manifold and prove that it is the exact nonlinear constraint for the complete attack space. With linearization and a small voltage angle assumption, the cycle manifold can be transformed to the constraint in weighted cycle space. • Limits and'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 5, 'text': 'or function-generator-driven coaxial coils [11]. Intercepting the field visibly extinguishes an indicator lamp on a receiver coil, demonstrating that shielding efficiency increases with frequency. Alternatively, a cage-like conductor arrangement comprising a central rod surrounded by a ring of parallel outer wires, each equipped with inline indicator lamps, visually contrasts current transport modes [7]: direct DC application illuminates all lamps uniformly, whereas high-frequency AC restricts current strictly to the outer perimeter, leaving the central wire dark. Further experimental demonstrations can be found in [12]. While effective as conceptual visual aids, none of these demonstrations enable students to quantitatively measure the actual change in the ohmic resistance of the conductor. To the best of our knowledge, quantitative RAC experiments on standard laboratory equipment are virtually absent from introductory physics collections. Dedicated high-frequency setups are dominated by inductive reactance, making it challenging to extract the small milliohm-scale resistance from the large reactive background. Furthermore, they typically require costly instrumentation, like lock-in amplifiers or precision impedance analyzers, that is rarely available for large student cohorts and risks acting as a “black box” that obscures the core electrodynamic mechanisms. To bridge this gap, this paper presents an accessible and cost-effective experimental approach that, beyond a'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 6, 'text': 'precision impedance analyzers, that is rarely available for large student cohorts and risks acting as a “black box” that obscures the core electrodynamic mechanisms. To bridge this gap, this paper presents an accessible and cost-effective experimental approach that, beyond a static DC baseline, operates exclusively at standard power-grid frequency (50 Hz). Rather than sweeping frequencies over several orders of magnitude with specialized high-frequency equipment, the relevant electrodynamic length scale, the skin depth δ, is tailored via the magnetic permeability of the specimen. By contrasting ferromagnetic structural steel with non-magnetic (austenitic) stainless steel, we induce a counterintuitive effect: the conductor of lower DC resistivity displays a pronounced AC impedance surge at line frequency, whereas the higher-resistance sample remains essentially unaltered. High-current excitation from standard variable AC/DC power supplies amplifies both DC and AC voltage drops into easily measurable millivolt signals, while a four-point Kelvin scheme eliminates parasitic contact resistances. Following a brief theoretical framework (Section 2), this paper presents the experimental setup (Section 3), quantitative results (Section 4), and pedagogical implications (Section 5). 2 Theoretical background This section outlines the electrodynamic foundation required to interpret our measurements. After establishing uniform DC conduction as a baseline, we analyze time-harmonic AC transport through'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 3, 'text': 'the tendency of an alternating electric current to distribute itself unevenly within a conductor, resulting in a higher current density near the surface that decays exponentially towards the core. This spatial redistribution effectively reduces the usable cross-sectional area. Consequently, the alternating-current (AC) resistance RAC increases with angular frequency ω above its static direct-current baseline RDC, yielding: RAC(ω) ≥RDC (1) with RAC(0) = RDC. Understanding this spatial current redistribution is essential not only for foundational electrodynamics, but also for practical engineering applications [6]. Most prominently, it dictates the design of high-frequency waveguides. In these hollow metallic pipes, electromagnetic wave propagation is driven solely by thin surface currents along the inner walls, making core material redundant. In power engineering, it dictates high-power busbar design, where heavy-duty conductors use flat or multi-leaf geometries to maximize surface area and minimize ohmic losses during high-current distribution. Finally, in manufacturing, it enables induction surface hardening, a process using alternating magnetic fields to heat and harden only the outer skin of a component while leaving its ductile core intact. Despite its technical importance, a survey of contemporary instructional literature reveals a striking asymmetry in how eddy currents and the skin effect are demonstrated in the classroom. Qualitative'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 19, 'text': 'of the solid cylindrical conductors and then analyzes the material-dependent AC impedance surge at line frequency (50 Hz), highlighting the role of permeability in driving the skin effect. 4.1 DC baseline and material resistivity Prior to investigating AC transport, a DC baseline measurement was established to verify Ohm’s law and determine the static electrical properties of the conductors (Equation (3)). Measurements were performed on solid rods of non-magnetic stainless steel and ferromagnetic structural steel with a conductive length l = 80.0 cm and rod radius r0 = 6.00 cm. As shown by the circular symbols in Figure 2, the DC U–I characteristics exhibit strictly linear behaviour passing through the origin. The slope directly yields the static DC resistance RDC (Equation (3)), obtained via linear fits with a fixed zero intercept. Stainless steel displays a significantly steeper DC slope (RDC = 5.19(1) mΩ) than structural steel (RDC = 1.39(1) mΩ). 5 Figure 2. Voltage–current (U–I) characteristics for solid cylindrical rods (l = 80.0 cm, r0 = 6.00 mm) under DC (circles) and 50 Hz AC excitation (diamonds). For non-magnetic stainless steel (red), DC and AC characteristics collapse onto a single line (|ZAC| ≈RDC). For ferromagnetic structural steel (blue), high permeability induces'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04326', 'title': 'Why the better conductor surges in impedance: a low-cost demonstration of the skin effect at line frequency', 'chunk_index': 17, 'text': 'internal inductance, accounting for the inductive reactance that arises under AC operation. (b) Practical laboratory implementation: the outer red crocodile clips inject the driving current from the variable source via the rheostat and the ammeter, while the inner black crocodile clips tap the potential drop across a defined gauge length directly into the voltmeter. The schematic layout and laboratory implementation are depicted in Figure 1(a) and (b), respectively. In the equivalent circuit, the conductor specimen is represented by a lumped resistor in series with an internal inductance. Under steady-state DC conditions, this inductance generates no voltage drop and does not affect the RDC measurement. Under AC operation, however, the internal inductive reactance can reach a magnitude comparable to the AC resistance, requiring consideration when modeling the overall electrical response. Connecting a voltage source directly across a milliohm specimen would create a near-short-circuit condition. To stabilize the current and prevent rapid Joule heating, a high-power slide rheostat (10 Ω, 5.7 A continuous rating; Indosaw PRN-322 / Contrex) is placed in series as a ballast resistor, converting the supply into a passive constant current source. Primary current scaling is controlled via the power supply, with the rheostat pre-set while de-energized to avoid'}]}

{'answer': "According to the provided context, cryptographic hash functions work by representing the bits of the input message as elements of a semigroup and multiplying the corresponding elements. This gives Cayley hash functions useful homomorphic properties, such as the homomorphic property HM(XY ) = HM(X) · HM(Y ) and the associativity property HM(XY Z) = HM(XY ) · HM(Z) = HM(X) · HM(Y Z).

In more detail, the context states that for a Cayley hash function, the function HM takes an arbitrary bit string X and returns the product of matrices A and B, denoted as HM(X). The homomorphic property HM(XY ) = HM(X) · HM(Y ) allows for the efficient computation of the hash of a concatenated message, without having to read the entire message first or know its length.

Additionally, the context mentions that the Cayley hash function has other useful properties, such as the ability to hash a stream of bits without knowing when it stops, and the ability to efficiently compute the hash of an amended document by hashing just the amended part and multiplying the result by the hash of the original document.

It's worth noting that the context does not provide a detailed explanation of how cryptographic hash functions work in general, but rather focuses on the specific properties and functionalities of Cayley hash functions, which are a type of cryptographic hash function.", 'status': 'success', 'error': None, 'token_usage': 3832, 'chunks': [{'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.04442', 'title': 'CHAMP: Cayley HAshing with Matrix Products', 'chunk_index': 1, 'text': 'CHAMP: Cayley HAshing with Matrix Products ALEXANDER DEMIN, ALEXEY OVCHINNIKOV, AND VLADIMIR SHPILRAIN Abstract. Cayley hash functions hash binary strings by representing the bits of the mes- sage as elements of a semigroup and multiplying the corresponding elements. This gives Cayley hash functions useful homomorphic properties. Here, we propose CHAMP, a Cayley hash function based on products of 2 by 2 matrices over a finite field, and discuss its security, implementation, and performance. 1. Introduction Hash functions are easy-to-compute compression functions that take a variable-length input and convert it to a fixed-length output. Hash functions are used as compact represen- tations, or digital fingerprints, of data and to provide message integrity. Cryptographic hash functions have many information-security applications, notably in digital signatures, mes- sage authentication codes, and other forms of authentication. Cryptographic hash functions should satisfy the following basic security requirements: (1) Collision resistance: it should be computationally infeasible to find two different inputs that hash to the same output. (2) Preimage resistance (sometimes called non-invertibility): it should be computation- ally infeasible to find an input which hashes to a specified output. We note that collision resistance also implies second preimage resistance: it should be computationally infeasible to find a'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.37342', 'title': 'Collision Detection is Instance \widetilde{O}
ptimal Under the Birthday Threshold', 'chunk_index': 24, 'text': 'particular input, although it must still be correct on every input. Generic collision search. Random-walk methods for finding collisions go back to Pollard’s rho method [34, 35]. Important refinements include Brent’s cycle-detection procedure [9] and the parallel collision search of van Oorschot and Wiener [43]. The probabilistic behavior of such walks is closely connected to random-mapping statistics [16]. Time–memory tradeoffs for function inversion provide another influential generic paradigm [26, 15], while Bellare and Kohno [6] quantify how nonuniform hash outputs affect birthday attacks. We note that these works generally do not consider the per-instance complexity, as opposed to this paper. Cryptographic and quantum collision notions. Collision resistance has also motivated refined definitions and attacks for hash functions, including keyless formulations [37], multicollision attacks 6 on iterated hashing [27], and constructions resilient to many collisions [30]. In the quantum query model, collision finding and element distinctness admit different complexity bounds from their classical counterparts [8, 1]. These lines are conceptually adjacent but not directly comparable to our result: our model is classical and Las Vegas, the function is an arbitrary fixed input rather than a random oracle or a cryptographic construction, and the benchmark is the instance-specific advantage supplied by unlabeled'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.31087', 'title': 'BenX: Resource-Sharing Permutations for Computational Integrity', 'chunk_index': 1, 'text': 'BenX: Resource-Sharing Permutations for Computational Integrity Luca Campa1 , Thomas De Cnudde2 , Al Kindi3, Arnab Roy4 , Fabian Schmid5 , Markus Schofnegger6 , and Stefano Trevisani7 1 campa1102@gmail.com 2 Fhenix, Tel Aviv, Israel thomas.decnudde@proton.me 3 Miden, Dubai, United Arab Emirates al.kindi@miden.team 4 arnab.roy@windowslive.com 5 Technical University of Graz, Graz, Austria fabian.schmid@tugraz.at 6 [[alloc] init], New York, USA markus.schofnegger@gmail.com 7 stefanotrevisani98@gmail.com Abstract. Cryptographic hash functions over integers modulo a prime play a decisive role in the efficiency and security of proof systems for computational integrity. Early designs focused on compact arithmetic circuits and efficient software execution, primarily targeting general-purpose CPUs rather than hardware accelerators. This work focuses on enabling efficient resource sharing and hardware acceleration alongside efficient software execution. We propose BenX, a permutation-based hash function designed for hardware acceler- ation, fast CPU execution, and low circuit complexity in proof systems. To construct the underlying permutation, we turn the Beneš network into an invertible function over F2 p using a Dickson polynomial. Its structure yields a permutation particularly suited to hardware resource sharing and acceleration. Fully pipelined on FPGA, BenX matches the throughput of Poseidon2, with 1.36× and 2.15× lower latency for Goldilocks and BabyBear, respectively. Although larger as a'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.31087', 'title': 'BenX: Resource-Sharing Permutations for Computational Integrity', 'chunk_index': 117, 'text': 'efficient representation proposed by the Poseidon paper [40, Appendix B]. The other design strategies exhibit familiar trade-offs. Hash functions relying on the power maps xd and x1/d incur a substantial plain evaluation cost for the inverse power, as seen for Rescue-Prime and Griffin. Hash functions based on the split-and-lookup paradigm, with non- linear layers built from non-algebraic bit transformations, achieve the lowest plain evaluation times in this comparison, but their efficient arithmetizations rely on lookup support that may not be available in all proof systems. 13 https://extgit.isec.tugraz.at/krypto/zkfriendlyhashzoo/'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.31087', 'title': 'BenX: Resource-Sharing Permutations for Computational Integrity', 'chunk_index': 82, 'text': '44th Annual International Conference on the Theory and Applications of Cryptographic Techniques, Madrid, Spain, May 4-8, 2025, Pro- ceedings, Part I. pp. 303–332. Lecture Notes in Computer Science, Springer (2025). https: //doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-91107-1_11 24 26. Campa, L., Roy, A., Steiner, M., Trevisani, S.: Arion: Arithmetization-oriented hashing for zero- knowledge proof systems. ToSC – IACR Transactions on Symmetric Cryptology (2026), https: //eprint.iacr.org/2026/1842 27. Canteaut, A., Beyne, T., Dinur, I., Eichlseder, M., Leander, G., Leurent, G., Plasencia, M.N., Perrin, L., Sasaki, Y., Todo, Y., Wiemer, F.: Report on the security of STARK-friendly hash functions (version 2.0). Technical Report (2020), https://eips.ethereum.org/assets/eip-5988/ papers/report_security_stark_friendly_hash.pdf 28. Cantor, D.G., Zassenhaus, H.: A new algorithm for factoring polynomials over finite fields. Mathematics of Computation 36(154), 587–592 (1981). https://doi.org/10.1090/ s0025-5718-1981-0606517-5 29. Chen, X., Yang, B., Zhu, W., Wang, H., Tao, Q., Yin, S., Zhu, M., Wei, S., Liu, L.: A high- performance ntt/msm accelerator for zero-knowledge proof using load-balanced fully-pipelined montgomery multiplier. IACR Transactions on Cryptographic Hardware and Embedded Systems (1), 275–313 (2025). https://doi.org/10.46586/tches.v2025.i1.275-313 30. Cox, D., D’Andrea, C.: Subresultants and the shape lemma. Math. Comput. 92(343), 2355–2379 (2023). https://doi.org/10.1090/MCOM/3840 31. Cox, D.A., Little, J., O’Shea, D.: Ideals, Varieties, and Algorithms – An Introduction to Compu- tational Algebraic Geometry and'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.04442', 'title': 'CHAMP: Cayley HAshing with Matrix Products', 'chunk_index': 4, 'text': 'is as follows. We start by describing additional functional- ities of Cayley hash functions in Section 2. In Section 3, we describe some of the previous proposals for platform (semi)groups as well as our own new proposal. In Section 4, we give a formal description of our hashing protocol and suggested parameters, as well as rationale for parameter selection. In Section 5, we give a Julia code for our protocol. In Section 6, we discuss security and possible attacks on our hash function. In Section 7, we give performance data and compare performance of our hash function to that of SHA-512. In Section 8, we report results of testing our hash function for pseudorandomness using the NIST statistical test suite. Finally, we conclude with some open problems in Section 9. 2. Additional functionalities of Cayley hash functions Cayley hash functions have some very useful properties that standard hash functions, like SHA family, do not have. We explain these properties below. In what follows, XY denotes concatenation of bit strings X and Y , and HM denotes the function that takes an arbitrary bit string to the corresponding product of matrices A and B. I. Homomorphic property HM(XY ) = HM(X)'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.04442', 'title': 'CHAMP: Cayley HAshing with Matrix Products', 'chunk_index': 36, 'text': 'applications to hashing, Groups, Complexity, Cryptology 8 (2016), 155–161. [17] V. Shpilrain, B. Sosnovski, Cayley hashing with cookies, in: Future of Information and Communication Conference (FICC 2025), Springer Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems 1284 (2025), 706–718. [18] J.-P. Tillich and G. Z´emor, Group-theoretic hash functions, in Proceedings of the First French-Israeli Workshop on Algebraic Coding, Lecture notes Comp. Sci. 781 (1994), 90–110. [19] J.-P. Tillich and G. Z´emor, Hashing with SL2, in CRYPTO 1994, Lecture Notes Comp. Sci. 839 (1994), 40–49. [20] J. von zur Gathen, I. E. Shparlinski, Generating safe primes, J. Math. Cryptol. 7 (2013), 333–365. [21] Yufei Yuan, Ruichen Wu, Shanpeng Wei, Junxu Shen, Jinpeng Liu, Yixin Zhang, Structural Analysis of Seven Hash Functions Submitted to the NGCC, preprint. https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/2152 [22] G. Z´emor, Hash functions and graphs with large girths, in: Eurocrypt’91, Lecture Notes in Comput. Sci. 547 (1991), 508–511. Laboratoire d’informatique de l’´Ecole polytechnique, LIX, UMR 7161, CNRS, 1 rue Honor´e d’Estienne d’Orves, 91120 Palaiseau, France Email address: alexander.demin@lix.polytechnique.fr Department of Mathematics, Queens College, City University of New York, Queens, NY 11367 Email address: alexey.ovchinnikov@qc.cuny.edu Department of Mathematics, The City College of New York, New York, NY 10031 Email address: shpilrain@yahoo.com'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.04442', 'title': 'CHAMP: Cayley HAshing with Matrix Products', 'chunk_index': 27, 'text': 'to keep in mind. 7. Performance We implemented our hash function in the 512-bit and 1024-bit output versions, each in two variants: reference and optimized implementations. The optimized implementation incorporates two main performance boosters. First, since the entries of A and B are small integers, we accumulate the entries of several consecutive matrix products using native machine integer types before reducing modulo p, thus using fewer multiple-precision arithmetic operations. Second, we use a look-up table that stores the hash value corresponding to each possible byte (256 entries in total); equivalently, the table contains all possible products of eight generators (each being either A or B). This allows the implementation to process the input byte-by-byte rather than bit-by-bit. In this section, we report experimental performance of the optimized implementation. Our benchmarking was performed in two different setups representing popular platforms: CHAMP: Cayley HAshing with Matrix Products 9 Figure 6.1. Bit length of the sum of absolute values of the entries of HM(X), the latter computed over Z without modular reduction, as a function of the bit length of the input X. The inputs X are chosen uniformly at random. Figure 6.2. Bit length of the sum of absolute values of the'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.31087', 'title': 'BenX: Resource-Sharing Permutations for Computational Integrity', 'chunk_index': 85, 'text': 'Legendre Symbol and the Modulo-2 Operator in Symmetric Schemes over Fnp Preimage Attack on Full Grendel. IACR Trans. Sym- metric Cryptol. 2022(1), 5–37 (2022). https://doi.org/10.46586/tosc.v2022.i1.5-37 42. Grassi, L., Khovratovich, D., Schofnegger, M.: Poseidon2: A faster version of the Poseidon hash function. In: Mrabet, N.E., Feo, L.D., Duquesne, S. (eds.) Progress in Cryptology – AFRICACRYPT 2023. Lecture Notes in Computer Science, vol. 14064, pp. 177–203. Springer (2023). https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-37679-5_8 43. Grassi, L., Khovratovich, D., Schofnegger, M.: Poseidon2: A faster version of the poseidon hash function. In: Progress in Cryptology - AFRICACRYPT 2023 - 14th International Con- ference on Cryptology in Africa, Sousse, Tunisia, July 19-21, 2023, Proceedings. Lecture Notes in Computer Science, vol. 14064, pp. 177–203. Springer (2023). https://doi.org/10.1007/ 978-3-031-37679-5_8, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-37679-5_8 44. Haböck, U., Levit, D., Papini, S.: Circle STARKs. Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2024/278 (2024), https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/278 45. Jain, R.: The number of solutions of a random system of polynomials over a finite field (2024). https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2409.06866 BenX: Resource-Sharing Permutations for Computational Integrity 25 46. Jakobsen, T., Knudsen, L.R.: The Interpolation Attack on Block Ciphers. In: FSE 1997. LNCS, vol. 1267, pp. 28–40 (1997). https://doi.org/10.1007/bfb0052332 47. Jean, J., Naya-Plasencia, M., Peyrin, T.: Improved Rebound Attack on the Finalist Grøstl. In: FSE 2012. LNCS,'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.04442', 'title': 'CHAMP: Cayley HAshing with Matrix Products', 'chunk_index': 5, 'text': 'In what follows, XY denotes concatenation of bit strings X and Y , and HM denotes the function that takes an arbitrary bit string to the corresponding product of matrices A and B. I. Homomorphic property HM(XY ) = HM(X) · HM(Y ) for any bit strings X, Y . II. Associativity property HM(XY Z) = HM(XY ) · HM(Z) = HM(X) · HM(Y Z) for any bit strings X, Y, Z. Why are these properties useful? In contrast with hash functions in the SHA family, Cayley hash functions have the following useful functionalities: 1. To hash a bit string X, one does not have to read the whole X first or even know the length of X; you can just hash X “as you go”, bit by bit. You can even hash a stream of bits without knowing when it stops. 2. When an already hashed document X is amended by Y , one does not have to hash the whole amended document XY all over again, but rather hash just the amended part Y and then multiply the result by the hash of the original document because HM(XY ) = HM(X)HM(Y ). Also note that one does not'}]}

{'answer': 'A recommendation algorithm chooses movies based on their genre, popularity, and other attributes such as in-degree and PageRank centrality measures. The algorithm bins movies into three categories: bottom bin (non-popular nodes), middle bin (moderately popular nodes), and top bin (very popular nodes). The lower the assortativity coefficient, the higher is the overall diversity. The algorithm also uses a novel quantification of diversity based on mixing patterns of the recommendation network, where movies are binned according to their genre. Additionally, the algorithm uses a contingency matrix based on popularity to visualize the relationships among genres.

The algorithm models the interaction of an individual user with the recommendation system as a random walk over the recommendation network (RN). The RN is created for the three movie recommendation systems (IMDb, Google Play, and Netﬂix), and the algorithm measures diversity and information segregation using this framework.

In summary, a recommendation algorithm chooses movies based on a combination of factors, including genre, popularity, and attribute-based binning, and uses a novel framework to measure diversity and information segregation.', 'status': 'success', 'error': None, 'token_usage': 3262, 'chunks': [{'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02710', 'title': 'A Network-centric Framework for Auditing Recommendation Systems', 'chunk_index': 6, 'text': 'recommendation list that is shown on the page of the item i. We refer to this network as the recommendation network.1 The different attributes of the items (e.g., genre or type of movies, topics of news articles, etc.) can be encoded as node properties. Note that the 1Note that the network is independent of the user information, and does not assume knowledge of user-item interactions. framework is different from the standard item-item similarity network, which essentially is a complete undirected graph and does not depend on recommendation outputs. At the same time this recommendation network has the ability to model the nudges that users, exposed to these digital environments, could experience. Contributions: We choose the movie domain for the purpose of our experiments. In particular, we apply our framework on three popular movie recommendation sites – IMDb (www.imdb.com), Google Play (play.google.com/store/movies), and Netﬂix (dvd.netﬂix.com). The motivation behind investigating the movie domain is driven by the recent claims of bias in the Recommendation Systems deployed at online movie sites such as Netﬂix2, which makes it important to audit movie recommendation systems. Based on the formulation of a recommendation network, we make the following contributions in this paper. • We put forward'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02710', 'title': 'A Network-centric Framework for Auditing Recommendation Systems', 'chunk_index': 21, 'text': 'of movies from different genres). The distribution of the different types / genres seen by the user, in the course of a walk, is henceforth referred to as the “observed distribution” of the user, which will be used to quantify the diversity of information that the user is exposed to. C. Datasets for applying the framework While the proposed framework can be applied to RSs in any domain (e.g., e-commerce, news media, social friendship recommendations), for the present work, we chose to apply it to movie recommendation systems. As outlined in the introduction, the choice is motivated by various factors. First, there are increasing concerns about various forms of bias in movie recommendation systems, which motivated us to audit movie RSs. Also, different movie recommendation sites index RN Nodes Edges Avg. Degree Reciprocating Edges IMDb 172,582 1,463,966 8.483 311,424 (21.27%) Google Play 2,143 40,663 18.975 12,107 (29.77%) Netﬂix 24,016 166,232 7.316 9,456 (5.66%) Table I: Statistics of Recommendation Networks of three popular movie RSs – IMDb, Google Play, and Netﬂix. the same universe of movies, thus making the comparison meaningful (whereas, in domains like online news, the news articles will differ widely between different sites). We choose three movie recommendation'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02710', 'title': 'A Network-centric Framework for Auditing Recommendation Systems', 'chunk_index': 22, 'text': 'three popular movie RSs – IMDb, Google Play, and Netﬂix. the same universe of movies, thus making the comparison meaningful (whereas, in domains like online news, the news articles will differ widely between different sites). We choose three movie recommendation platforms – (i) IMDb (www.imdb.com), (ii) Google Play (play.google.com/store/movies), and (iii) Netﬂix DVD rental service (dvd.netﬂix.com) – for the present study. Note that the last two are online service providers while the ﬁrst one is an online database. These choices allow us to investigate how the two media service providers compare in diversity and information segregation compared to the database. Each of these websites show a ranked list of recommen- dations on the page of every movie. We designed snowball sampling (BFS) crawlers for each of these websites. We seeded the crawler with an initial movie, crawled all recommenda- tions shown on the page of the seed movie, and pushed the recommendations to a queue, and repeated the process on the items in the queue. We continued the crawls till the queue was exhausted, to ensure that we collect the whole universe of items. The total number of movies whose data we could collect from the three sites are shown'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.17561', 'title': 'Structured Conformal Inference for Matrix Completion with Applications to Group Recommender Systems', 'chunk_index': 2, 'text': 'weighted conformalization framework that addresses the lack of exchangeability arising from structured calibration, introducing several innovations to overcome associated computational challenges. We demonstrate the practicality and effectiveness of our approach through extensive numerical experiments and an analysis of the MovieLens 100K dataset. Keywords: Collaborative filtering; confidence regions; conformal inference; exchangeability; Laplace’s method; simultaneous inference. 1 Introduction 1.1 Uncertainty Estimation for Group Recommender Systems Group recommender systems generate product suggestions collectively for groups of users [1], ex- tending traditional algorithms for individual recommendations. As digital interactions increasingly emphasize shared experiences, these systems are becoming more significant across various domains. For example, in the context of movies, they can help friends select a film for a movie night [2] and enhance user experiences on Teleparty—a platform that enables people to watch films together in 1Department of Statistics, University of California Irvine, Irvine, CA, USA. 2Department of Data Sciences and Operations, University of Southern California, Los Angeles, CA, USA. 3Business School, University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China. 4Department of Computer Science, University of Southern California, Los Angeles, CA, USA. *The first two authors contributed equally to this work. 1 arXiv:2404.17561v2 [stat.ME] 10 Feb 2025 sync—by suggesting content that aligns with the interests'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02710', 'title': 'A Network-centric Framework for Auditing Recommendation Systems', 'chunk_index': 30, 'text': 'a natural choice to bin the movies according to their genres. As stated in the dataset section, Google Play speciﬁes 15 different genres for movies, while IMDb and Netﬂix specify 29 different genres. (ii) Popularity: A natural choice for measuring the popularity of movies would be the ratings or number of views; however, not all movie recommendation sites provide these statistics. Hence, we use network centrality measures as estimates of the popularity of a node (movie). To this end, we consider two centrality measures - (1) in-degree, and (2) PageRank. We compute both the centrality scores over the RN and normalize to the range [0.0, 1.0]; this normalization is needed to compare the values across networks of very different sizes. We group Binning attribute IMDb Google Play Netﬂix Genre 0.1453 0.3983 0.0729 In-degree 0.1418 0.1687 0.0314 PageRank 0.1575 0.2525 0.0407 Table III: Assortativity coefﬁcients of the Recommendation Networks. The lower the assortativity coefﬁcient, the higher is the overall diversity. the movies into three bins – (i) bottom bin, containing non- popular nodes (movies) whose centrality is in the range [0.0, 0.2], (ii) middle bin, containing moderately popular nodes whose centrality lies in (0.2, 0.4], and (iii) top bin, containing very'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02710', 'title': 'A Network-centric Framework for Auditing Recommendation Systems', 'chunk_index': 7, 'text': 'the Recommendation Systems deployed at online movie sites such as Netﬂix2, which makes it important to audit movie recommendation systems. Based on the formulation of a recommendation network, we make the following contributions in this paper. • We put forward novel quantiﬁcations of diversity. The ﬁrst among these is based on mixing patterns of the rec- ommendation network [5], where the movies are binned based on their genre. We observe that IMDb and Netﬂix present higher diversity than Google Play. Some of the interesting ﬁndings are that for Google Play, the movies in 7 genres - ‘comedy’, ‘animation’, ‘action and adventure’, ‘Indian cinema’, ‘horror’ and ‘documentary’ - almost always recommend movies of the same genre itself. For IMDb, barring the two genres ‘adult’ and ‘reality TV’, the recommendations from all other genres are quite diverse and take the user to many other genres. Similar is the case for Netﬂix. • The second set of measures is based on graph-based notions of popularity like in-degree and PageRank. If one bins the items based on in-degree or PageRank, one observes an universal phenomena across all the three websites – the top bins have very diverse set of recommendations, often leading to the'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.12034', 'title': 'Conformal Group Recommender System', 'chunk_index': 9, 'text': 'recommend music at a particular time. Let’s browse [15] suggests web pages to a group consisting of two or more individuals who are likely to have shared interests.PolyLens [16] employs collaborative filtering and recommends movies to groups of users. Jameson et al. [17] proposed the Travel Decision Forum system, which helps groups plan vacation. The system provides an interface where the group members can view and copy other users’ preferences and combine them using the median strategy once all agree. Garcia et al. [18] proposed intersection and aggregation mechanisms to build a group profile from the individual profiles. Kagita et al.[19] use a virtual user approach with precedence mining to extend PRS to GRS. All the GRS approaches described so far utilize aggregation techniques to determine group profiles or recommendations, and they 3 are inept at associating confidence with the recommendation. In this proposal, we adopt our previous work [19] and propose a conformal group recommender system that furnishes confidence in the recommendations to improve the system’s reliability and user satisfaction. Another difference between the previous work [19] and our current proposal is that we have used coexistence in place of precedence mining, as in many real-world applications, the associations'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02710', 'title': 'A Network-centric Framework for Auditing Recommendation Systems', 'chunk_index': 33, 'text': 'the Google Play RSs (Figure 2a). For Google Play, it can be observed from Figure 2a that movies in 7 genres - ‘comedy’, ‘animation’, ‘action and adventure’, ‘Indian cin- ema’, ‘horror’ and ‘documentary’ - almost always recommend movies of the same genre; hence the recommendations lack diversity. For IMDb, Figure 2b suggests that barring the two genres ‘adult’ and ‘reality TV’, the recommendations from all other genres are quite diverse and lead to many other genres as well. The Netﬂix RSs (Figure 2c) also has good diversity. Also note that movies of few speciﬁc genres particularly rec- ommend movies of some other speciﬁc genres. For instance, in Netﬂix, ‘War’ movies mostly recommend ‘adult’ movies, while ‘mystery’ movies often recommend ‘ﬁlm-noir’ movies. Analyzing the contingency matrices of the RN is a good way of identifying such relationships among genres. (ii) Contingency matrix based on popularity: Binning with re- spect to in-degree and PageRank centrality measures yielded similar observations for all the RSs; hence we report results for binning based on in-degree. Figure 3 visualizes the con- tingency matrices of the RNs based on popularity (in-degree). The stacked bars show the fraction of outward transitions (recommendations) going from items in one bin'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02710', 'title': 'A Network-centric Framework for Auditing Recommendation Systems', 'chunk_index': 47, 'text': 'number of movies). There- fore, even though two groups A and B have been exposed to the same number of information units (genres), i.e., their evenness is the same, their concentration can be different because the exposed units can span different proportions of the information space. B. Using proposed framework to measure information segre- gation As stated earlier, we model the interaction of an individual user with the RSs as a random walk over the RN. We divide a population of users into several groups, based on their starting movie (node) and propensity to follow the recommendations (teleportation probability tp). All users in a particular group start from the same movie, and have the same tp. To model users having many different starting points and tp, we do the following. We select ten movies from the all- time top-50 movies of IMDb [24] as starting nodes for the random walks. The reason behind selecting popular movies is that we assume most users are likely to start browsing an online movie site with a popular movie (and will then follow recommendations for further exploration for N = 400). Also, we select only those movies as starting points that are present in'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02710', 'title': 'A Network-centric Framework for Auditing Recommendation Systems', 'chunk_index': 25, 'text': 'Adu Dra Wes 0.0 0.1 0.2 0.3 0.4 Fraction of Movies Observed (b) IMDb Figure 1: Observed distributions for random walks starting from the movie “The Godfather” over different RNs. Length of walk N = 400 steps and tp = 0.0 (always following recommendations). D. Recommendation networks for movie RSs We create the RN for the three movie RSs, and report some basic statistics in Table I. IMDb has the largest set of movies, followed by Netﬂix and Google Play. However, Google Play has the highest average node-degree. To demonstrate the result of the random walk process described in Section III-B, Figure 1 shows the observed distributions of random walks starting from the movie ‘The Godfather’ over Google Play and IMDb RNs. Both walks are for N = 400 steps and tp = 0.0 (always following recommendations). We ﬁnd that the observed distribution in Google Play is dominated by one genre (‘Action’, which is one of the genres dominating the product base), while that in IMDb is much more evenly distributed across genres. In the rest of this paper, we apply the RN framework on the three aforementioned movie RSs, to measure diversity (Section IV) and information segregation (Section V).'}]}

{'answer': "The context does not provide a detailed explanation of how compiler optimization works. However, it mentions that Presage uses a compiler-based approach to perform software prefetching, and that it builds on top of the LLVM compiler infrastructure.

According to the context, the compiler analysis phase is the first stage of the methodology, which provides the necessary information to guide the subsequent steps of the automation methodology. This suggests that the compiler is used to analyze the application and generate the necessary information for optimization.

Unfortunately, the provided context does not provide enough information to provide a comprehensive explanation of how compiler optimization works. If you're looking for more information on this topic, I recommend searching for additional resources or articles that provide a more detailed explanation of compiler optimization and its various techniques.", 'status': 'success', 'error': None, 'token_usage': 3625, 'chunks': [{'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.22636', 'title': 'Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments', 'chunk_index': 46, 'text': 'as a compiler pass [6, 9, 19, 30, 54, 55, 77, 82, 90]. Subsequent work [52, 56] utilizes dynamic profiling to discover more effective prefetch candidates. APT-GET [42] improves upon these by utilizing profiling to compute opti- mal lookahead distances. Finally, RPG2 [93] injects and tunes software prefetches online, the first work to acknowledge that prefetches must be trialed and errored. Hardware Prefetching. Simple strided- or pattern-based prefetchers [22, 35, 37, 46, 61, 65, 66, 78] are ubiquitous in Matthew Giordano, Parthasarathy Ranganathan, Baris Kasikci, and Akanksha Jain modern processors. Some novel, more complex prefetchers have been implemented [38, 88, 89], but their effectiveness is less clear [41]. While alternative, run-ahead designs have been proposed [10, 20, 24, 33, 48, 53, 62, 63, 74], the prohibi- tive hardware changes preclude their implementation. Programmable Hardware Prefetching. Several works [7, 17, 43, 45, 67, 85, 91, 92] propose programmable hard- ware prefetchers.However, these require both hardware and software changes to use effectively. AI Optimizers. AlphaEvolve [69] rewrites algorithms to meet some optimization metric. ECO [51] searches for and performs code optimizations that are known to be beneficial. Our work combines the two approaches, performing local code optimizations via software prefetching. 7 Conclusion'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.06291', 'title': 'Acceleration of Data Analytics on Heterogeneous Supercloud Systems', 'chunk_index': 10, 'text': 'on general approaches using LLVM static analysis and dynamic profiling [19], or targeting specific parallelism types [20]. However, they do not address diverse computation models which can be used to enhance performance and they focus mainly on streaming applications. Design space exploration (DSE) methods have also been explored [21–23]. They focus on strategies for synthesizing accelerators and implementing optimizations in terms of performance improvement, accuracy, and reducing the area required for hardware implementations. Nevertheless, they target mainly FPGA devices and lack a potentially wider variety of hardware. 3 Compiler Tool-chain To address the challenges mentioned in the previous section, we build on ReDSEa (Recursive Design Space Exploration Automation): the tool-chain we introduced in [1], based on the LLVM [24] Compiler Infrastructure (Version 14.0.0). 3.1 Compiler Analysis The first stage of our methodology is analyzing the application that is going to be mapped to a heterogeneous system or cloud architecture. The analysis phase includes an automated process that provides our compiler infrastructure with necessary information so as to guide the subsequent steps (Cost Models and Design Space Exploration) of the automation methodology. It can be viewed as the input to our automated system. The analysis is performed by generating the respective'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.22636', 'title': 'Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments', 'chunk_index': 12, 'text': 'such accesses, achieving speedups close to manually inserted prefetches on benchmarks where indirect accesses dominate. Though not the first compiler-based software prefetch injection [9, 19, 30, 54, 55, 77, 82, 90], they most recently have found novel speedups with this approach. APT-GET [42] augments this approach with feedback- directed optimization (FDO): profiling the workload under Figure 3. Effective SPEC26 graph500 prefetch patch found manually, providing a 3.5% speedup on training input. execution to compute prefetch locations and lookahead dis- tances. RPG2 [93] extends APT-GET by inserting and tuning prefetches while programs run. After a short LLC-miss profiling win- dow (2 seconds) to determine insertion location and type, RPG2 uses BOLT [71] to inject prefetches into the binary for load PCs that match a prefetchable pattern. It then runs a live analysis loop that tunes lookahead distances to maximize IPC. Importantly, RPG2 introduces the idea that prefetches can often hurt performance and must be trialed. Like prior work, it uses heuristics to detect prefetchable loops, which often fail on real-world workloads (see Section 5.5.3). 3 Motivation 3.1 Why Agents? The search space of software prefetching opportunities is vast: Every load can, in theory, be prefetched under various conditions and with many'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.22636', 'title': 'Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments', 'chunk_index': 47, 'text': 'AI Optimizers. AlphaEvolve [69] rewrites algorithms to meet some optimization metric. ECO [51] searches for and performs code optimizations that are known to be beneficial. Our work combines the two approaches, performing local code optimizations via software prefetching. 7 Conclusion We introduce Presage, which uses LLM semantic understand- ing to search the space of software prefetches. Through a multi-agent harness that proposes, optimizes, and com- bines prefetch candidates, Presage discovers effective soft- ware prefetches and transformations around them, yielding a geomean 10% runtime reduction across 81 workloads. Acknowledgements Generative AI was utilized to aid in creating Presage, to aid in writing visualization scripts, to aid in finding references, and to edit and fix grammar and spelling. References [1] 2026. https://www.spec.org/cpu2026/ [2] Andreas Abel, Yuying Li, Richard O’Grady, Chris Kennelly, and Dar- ryl Gove. 2024. A Profiling-Based Benchmark Suite for Warehouse- Scale Computers. In 2024 IEEE International Symposium on Perfor- mance Analysis of Systems and Software (ISPASS). 325–327. doi:10. 1109/ISPASS61541.2024.00046 [3] Advanced Micro Devices, Inc. 2026. AMD64 Architecture Programmer’s Manual, Volume 2: System Programming. Advanced Micro Devices, Inc. https://docs.amd.com/v/u/en-US/24593_3.45_APM_Vol2_PUB Publication No. 24593. [4] Masab Ahmad, Farrukh Hijaz, Qingchuan Shi, and Omer Khan. 2015. CRONO: A Benchmark Suite for Multithreaded Graph Algorithms'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.22636', 'title': 'Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments', 'chunk_index': 5, 'text': 'present in the search fringe. Prior work leverages feedback-directed optimization to insert software prefetches [42, 93], and while effective on a limited class of workloads, these solutions suffer from three drawbacks. First, they rely on hand-crafted heuristics to match code patterns to software prefetching opportunities, and these heuristics do not generalize to a broader class of workloads. For example, RPG2—a state-of-the-art software prefetch insertion tool [93]—is unable to find any software prefetching opportunities in 73% of SPEC CPU 2026 [1] work- loads because it cannot identify matches for the exact pat- terns that it was designed for. Across our entire evaluation suite, RPG2 can only operate on 24 out of 106 workload-input pairs, and only discovers speedups on 17 of those. Second, these heuristics do not capture complex software prefetch- ing opportunities that require understanding the interplay between multiple variables, such as the one highlighted in Figure 1. More broadly, there are many different transforma- tions around code that make prefetches more or less effective that prior work does not explore. Third, we find that suc- cessful individual prefetches do not naively scale to large codebases with multiple hot loops due to the complexity of how prefetches interact with one'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.06291', 'title': 'Acceleration of Data Analytics on Heterogeneous Supercloud Systems', 'chunk_index': 9, 'text': 'lack automated tools for performance estimation based on hardware resources and granularity level to achieve maximum throughput. The second stage focuses on the SVD of the bidiagonal matrix. Gates et al. [12] use the Divide and Conquer (D&C) algorithm, achieving better performance compared to QR factorization methods [16]. They accelerate matrix multiplications with GPUs, while the CPU handles secular equation solving and singular vector computation. Liu et al. [13] also use the Divide and Conquer approach but on the top of that, they offload the secular equation computation to accelerators. They examine the performance of different algorithms for this task and they try to reduce the number of the floating point operations of the different algorithms in order to decrease latency. However, these papers do not delve into the recursion depth of the Divide and Conquer algorithm, a crucial aspect in heterogeneous systems. Additionally, compiler-based methodologies have been introduced for automatic performance estimation in hetero- geneous systems [17, 18], focusing on general approaches using LLVM static analysis and dynamic profiling [19], or targeting specific parallelism types [20]. However, they do not address diverse computation models which can be used to enhance performance and they focus mainly on streaming applications. Design'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.22636', 'title': 'Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments', 'chunk_index': 43, 'text': 'codependent variables, while optimizer 4 did the same with 11 variables in a different function. Opti- mizers 2 and 3 failed to discover a speedup after attempting various strategies to reduce instruction overhead in their hotspots. Ultimately, the combiner function explored the baseline, prefetch proposal 1, prefetch proposal 4, and then their combination, before ultimately realizing that prefetch proposal 1 alone was best. 5.6.2 xz Result. Figure 12 shows an effective prefetch for the SPEC 801.xz_s workload, providing a 0.7% runtime reduc- tion on training input. This compression workload involves the usage of hash tables and binary search trees for caching compressed data. Presage learns this through analyzing the workload and adds prefetches to hash table data for future iterations, including inserting all the necessary hash com- putations and gating that future iterations would perform. Overall, this example showcases several transformations, in- cluding codependent prefetches, array length gates, related variable gates, and complex math. 5.7 Cost and Breakdown Analysis Table 4 details a breakdown of Presage optimizations across all four evaluated suites. Notably, Presage provides optimiza- tion at a relatively cheap rate; each run averages a few dollars of cost. Each successful result averages a patch file of length 41.'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.22636', 'title': 'Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments', 'chunk_index': 44, 'text': '4 details a breakdown of Presage optimizations across all four evaluated suites. Notably, Presage provides optimiza- tion at a relatively cheap rate; each run averages a few dollars of cost. Each successful result averages a patch file of length 41. Presage explores around 30 different configurations of prefetches per run over the course of a few hours. In our ob- servation, when baseline runtime is high (e.g., SPEC, which takes many minutes), evaluation completely dominates over- all runtime. However, on quicker benchmarks (e.g., CRONO, which takes 30 seconds), a majority of time is spent waiting on LLM inferences between workload calls (e.g., reasoning, parsing perf data, reading and editing files). We also present Table 4. Breakdown statistics of Presage. All numbers are arithmetic means, except for runtime reduction, which is a geometric mean of runtime ratios converted into a percent- age improvement. Note that optimization time is inflated due to a configuration error. Efficiency means the percent- age of optimizer runs that beat the incumbent, averaged per optimizer. Presage discovers speedups on a majority of work- loads for a cost of a few dollars per deployment. A&J CRONO FleetBench SPEC26 N 3 37 7 34 Patch Generated 100.0% 83.8% 85.7%'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.22636', 'title': 'Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments', 'chunk_index': 64, 'text': 'and Parthasarathy Ran- ganathan. 2025. LLM-Box : An Agentic Framework for Guided Black-Box Optimization in Mapping LLMs onto Specialized Hard- ware Accelerators. In Machine Learning for Systems 2025. https: //openreview.net/forum?id=W2htGuo9de [73] Jason Jong Kyu Park, Yongjun Park, and Scott Mahlke. 2015. Fine Grain Cache Partitioning Using Per-Instruction Working Blocks. In Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments 2015 International Conference on Parallel Architecture and Compilation (PACT). 305–316. doi:10.1109/PACT.2015.11 [74] T. Ramirez, A. Pajuelo, O.J. Santana, and M. Valero. 2008. Runahead Threads to improve SMT performance. In 2008 IEEE 14th Interna- tional Symposium on High Performance Computer Architecture. 149–158. doi:10.1109/HPCA.2008.4658635 [75] Parthasarathy Ranganathan, Daniel Stodolsky, Jeff Calow, Jeremy Dorfman, Marisabel Guevara, Clinton Wills Smullen IV, Aki Ku- usela, Raghu Balasubramanian, Sandeep Bhatia, Prakash Chauhan, Anna Cheung, In Suk Chong, Niranjani Dasharathi, Jia Feng, Brian Fosco, Samuel Foss, Ben Gelb, Sara J. Gwin, Yoshiaki Hase, Da-ke He, C. Richard Ho, Roy W. Huffman Jr., Elisha Indupalli, Indira Ja- yaram, Poonacha Kongetira, Cho Mon Kyaw, Aaron Laursen, Yuan Li, Fong Lou, Kyle A. Lucke, JP Maaninen, Ramon Macias, Maire Mahony, David Alexander Munday, Srikanth Muroor, Narayana Penukonda, Eric Perkins-Argueta, Devin Persaud, Alex Ramirez, Ville-Mikko Rautio, Yolanda Ripley, Amir Salek, Sathish Sekar, Sergey N. Sokolov,'}, {'article_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.22636', 'title': 'Presage: Prefetch Search via Agent-Guided Experiments', 'chunk_index': 24, 'text': 'prefetch PKI), hypothesize potential improvements, and re− evaluate. 4. Leave code in optimal state. # Output Return a `Proposal` with success status, code diff, and runtime/MPKI delta vs. baseline. Figure 7. Prefetch Optimizer Agent Prompt. The optimizer repeatedly inserts prefetches, runs the workload, and ana- lyzes profiling metrics. Prefetch Optimizer Skill. We write a prefetch optimizer skill which seeds the agent with various ideas on how to effec- tively perform software prefetching. We find that this knowl- edge helps reduce optimization time and avoids wasted con- text on simple errors. This skill first defines software prefetch- ing syntax and instructs agents to first try prefetching into the L1d. It also instructs agents to discover effective prefetch insertion points before tuning exact lookahead distances, and mandates power-of-two lookahead distances to prevent hundreds of fine-tuning runs.Additionally, it strongly recom- mends inspecting the disassembly-level performance metrics of the hotspot after each run to observe new bottlenecks or overheads. Finally, this skill details some hints in discovering effective prefetches, listed below. • Gate prefetches (and try alternate predicates) when instruction overhead is high. • If gating itself is costly, try splitting the loop into an unconditional prefetch loop and a loop epilogue. • For'}]}
